      <p class="lead"><strong>Every infrared sauna on the market sits far below the international safety limit for magnetic fields. The real question is whether a brand's "low EMF" number means anything.</strong> Most don't. The term isn't regulated, and nearly every number traces back to a test the brand paid for. This guide covers what the actual standards say, and how to judge any EMF claim you run into.</p>

      <div class="call evidence">
        <div class="h">&#10003; Why this page exists</div>
        <p>AI search answers for sauna EMF questions tell readers to look for independent, third-party testing. Then they cite reports the brands commissioned themselves. This page won't hand you a number. It gives you the tools to judge the numbers you're already seeing.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="problem">The problem with every EMF number you've seen</h2>
      <p class="sub">A 65x spread for products all marketed as "low EMF."</p>

      <p>Search "infrared sauna EMF levels" and you'll find claims from 0.3 mG to over 20 mG. That's roughly a 65x spread, for products all sold as "low EMF." Nearly every specific number traces back to one of two places:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>The manufacturer's own marketing copy</strong>, with no test report attached.</li>
        <li><strong>A "third-party test report"</strong> that the brand commissioned and hosts itself. Vitatech Electromagnetics is the lab name that recurs most often. Sunlighten, Good Health Saunas, and Vortex Saunas all publish brand-specific Vitatech reports on their own sites.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>A real lab with real equipment doesn't make a test independent. The brand still chose when to test, which unit to test, and whether to publish. No one currently tests across brands with one consistent protocol and publishes every result, good or bad.</p>

      <h2 id="low-emf">What "low EMF" actually means (and doesn't)</h2>
      <p class="sub">It's not a regulated term.</p>

      <p>"Low EMF" has no certification body, no legal threshold, and no standard test position that brands agree on. EMF inside a sauna cabin is really three separate things:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Magnetic field.</strong> Created by current running through heater wiring. Measured in milligauss (mG) or microtesla (µT). 1 µT equals 10 mG.</li>
        <li><strong>Electric field.</strong> Created around energized wires. Measured in volts per meter (V/m).</li>
        <li><strong>RF (radiofrequency).</strong> Comes from the controller electronics and any Wi-Fi or Bluetooth module.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Most of the field you actually sit in comes from wiring layout and controller design, not the infrared heater itself. So "low EMF" is really a claim about electrical engineering. A "Low EMF Certified" sticker tells you almost nothing without a number, a measurement position, and a named meter.</p>

      <h2 id="standards">What the safety standards actually say</h2>
      <p class="sub">The numbers people quote come from very different sources, built for very different jobs.</p>

      <p>Sauna marketing tends to blend several standards together as if they measured the same thing. They don't. Here's where each common number comes from, all for the power-frequency (50 to 60 Hz) magnetic fields that sauna wiring produces.</p>

      <div class="tbl-wrap">
        <table class="cmp">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Source</th><th>Number</th><th>What it actually is</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>ICNIRP (2010)</strong></td>
              <td>200 µT (2,000 mG)</td>
              <td>International reference level for general public exposure. Set to prevent known, acute effects like nerve stimulation. Widely adopted in Europe.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>U.S. federal</strong></td>
              <td>None</td>
              <td>The EPA states there are no federal standards limiting these fields. A few states set limits at the edge of power-line rights-of-way. None apply to consumer products like saunas.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>IARC (2002)</strong></td>
              <td>0.3 to 0.4 µT (3 to 4 mG)</td>
              <td>Not a limit. IARC classed power-frequency magnetic fields as "possibly carcinogenic" (Group 2B). That rests on studies linking long-term average home exposure above this level to childhood leukemia. Animal studies didn't support the link.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Swedish TCO / MPR II</strong></td>
              <td>About 0.2 µT (2 mG)</td>
              <td>Emission standards for computer equipment, measured 30 to 50 cm from the device. TCO itself says these are not health-based exposure limits. This is usually the source of the "Swedish 2 to 3 mG" figure in sauna marketing.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Building biology guidance</strong></td>
              <td>Often under 1 mG</td>
              <td>Precautionary recommendations, mostly written for sleeping areas. Not regulatory, and not built around short sessions.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Put simply, every sauna passes the only binding international limit by a wide margin. The low numbers in marketing come from precautionary guidance or from computer-monitor standards. Those are useful reference points. They are not safety limits, and a sauna that exceeds them isn't breaking any rule.</p>

      <h2 id="why-hard">Why saunas don't fit neatly into any standard</h2>
      <p class="sub">Distance, duration, and position change everything.</p>

      <p>Magnetic fields drop off sharply with distance. A reading taken an inch from a heater panel can be many times higher than one taken where your torso actually sits. A reading taken a few feet away can be many times lower. That's why a number without a stated position is close to meaningless.</p>

      <p>Duration matters too. The IARC association is about long-term average exposure, around the clock, at home. A sauna session is 15 to 45 minutes of close exposure, a few times a week. No major standard is written for that pattern. Anyone who tells you exactly how a sauna session maps onto a 24-hour average limit is making an assumption, not citing a rule.</p>

      <h2 id="how-to-read">How to read any EMF claim you encounter</h2>
      <p class="sub">Five questions to ask before trusting any number.</p>

      <ul class="mistakes">
        <li><b>Who commissioned the test?</b> If the brand paid for and published the report, treat the number as marketing, not verification. That holds no matter how credible the lab sounds.</li>
        <li><b>Was the whole sauna tested, or just a heater?</b> Some reports measure a bare heater panel outside the cabin. The wiring, controller, and other panels that shape the real field aren't included. Ask for readings from an assembled, running sauna.</li>
        <li><b>Where was the meter positioned?</b> Panel-surface and floor readings don't reflect your exposure. Ask for readings at seated chest height, where you actually sit.</li>
        <li><b>What meter was used, and when was it calibrated?</b> A number with no named instrument or calibration date can't be checked.</li>
        <li><b>Which standard is the number compared to?</b> "Below the Swedish standard" usually means a computer-monitor emission spec. "Below ICNIRP" is true of every sauna. Neither tells you much on its own.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Skepticism is also warranted for round, marketing-friendly numbers ("0.3 mG!") with no method attached. Real readings vary by unit, position, and even nearby electronics.</p>

      <div class="call tip">
        <div class="h">&#9432; Want to check a sauna yourself?</div>
        <p>Consumer gaussmeters are affordable and widely available. If you measure your own sauna, take readings at seated chest height with the sauna running, and note the meter model. Compare your number to the table above, knowing what each reference point does and doesn't mean.</p>
      </div>

